## Problem

Draft models are typically trained with divergence objectives that match the target’s full distribution at a given context. Speculative-decoding acceptance, however, depends on the probabilities that the draft and target assign to the proposed tokens—usually drawn from a small set of plausible candidates. Neither forward nor reverse KL directly supervises the relative spacing among these candidates. Forward KL encourages broad coverage, whereas reverse KL is more mode-seeking; in both cases, the margins that influence acceptance emerge only indirectly from the overall distribution-matching objective.

## Novelty: Supervising How the Output Shifts

We introduce a logit-shift loss that supervises not only the draft’s output at the ground-truth context, but also how that output changes when a single token in the context is replaced. This objective directly constrains how the draft re-ranks plausible continuations after its context changes—a behavior that standard single-context distillation does not explicitly supervise. The resulting draft produces longer accepted prefixes and higher throughput with no additional inference cost.

## Motivation

Draft models are commonly trained using objectives such as forward KL, cross-entropy, or reverse KL, which fit the draft’s distribution to the target’s over the entire vocabulary at a given context. Acceptance is determined differently: each proposed token is evaluated using the probabilities assigned to it by the draft and target at that position. In practice, the proposed token usually belongs to a small set of plausible candidates. Because most vocabulary items carry negligible probability, the overall divergence can be low even when the draft misjudges the relative margins among these leading candidates.

Matching these margins at the ground-truth context is not sufficient because they can depend strongly on the preceding token. Replacing that token with another plausible candidate may leave the general set of possible continuations largely unchanged while substantially altering which continuation is preferred and by how much. Standard distillation at the ground-truth context constrains the margins there, but it does not explicitly supervise how they should change under such a substitution. This matters during autoregressive drafting, where the draft conditions on its own proposed tokens.

We therefore supervise this change directly. Running the target on both the original and substituted contexts and subtracting their logits gives the target’s logit shift, which captures how it re-ranks possible continuations after the preceding token changes. Because the two contexts differ by only one plausible token, much of their shared output structure cancels in the subtraction, emphasizing the change induced by the substitution. Training the draft to reproduce this shift provides supervision that is absent from a single-context objective. The shift loss is added alongside the standard distillation objective rather than replacing it.

## Method

**Substituted context.** At position (t), let (a_t) denote the draft’s top-1 prediction conditioned on the prefix (x_{<t}):

[
a_t = \arg\max_v z^D_v(x_{<t}).
]

We apply a substitution when

[
a_t \neq \arg\max_v z^T_v(x_{<t})
\quad\text{and}\quad
a_t \in \operatorname{top}\text{-}k!\left(z^T(x_{<t})\right),
\qquad k=3.
]

The resulting substituted context is

[
x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t}
========================

(x_1,\ldots,x_{t-1},a_t),
]

whereas the original context is

[
x_{\le t}
=========

(x_1,\ldots,x_{t-1},x_t).
]

The first condition ensures that the draft’s proposal differs from the target’s preferred token. The second restricts the substitution to a token that the target also considers plausible, keeping the two contexts close and focusing the objective on alternatives that the draft might actually propose.

**Logit-shift loss.** Both models are evaluated on the original and substituted contexts. Define centered logits as

[
\operatorname{center}(z)
========================

z-\frac{1}{|V|}\sum_{v\in V}z_v.
]

The target and draft shifts are

[
\Delta_T
========

## \operatorname{center}!\left(z^T(x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t})\right)

\operatorname{center}!\left(z^T(x_{\le t})\right),
]

[
\Delta_D
========

## \operatorname{center}!\left(z^D(x^{\mathrm{sub}}_{\le t})\right)

\operatorname{center}!\left(z^D(x_{\le t})\right).
]

We minimize

[
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{shift}}
============================

\left\lVert \Delta_D-\Delta_T \right\rVert_2^2,
]

and combine it with the standard distillation objective:

[
\mathcal{L}
===========

\mathcal{L}*{\mathrm{reg}}
+
\lambda \mathcal{L}*{\mathrm{shift}},
\qquad
\lambda=0.1,
]

where (\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}) is left unchanged.

**Why center the logits?** For any constant (c), the logits (z) and (z+c\mathbf{1}) define the same distribution under softmax. This additive component therefore contains no probability-relevant information for the draft to match. Centering removes it and retains only changes that can affect the output distribution.

**Why supervise the shift rather than the substituted output?** The original and substituted contexts differ by only one plausible token, so their output logits contain substantial shared structure. Directly distilling the full output at the substituted context would therefore largely repeat supervision already provided by (\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}). Taking the difference cancels this shared component and isolates the change caused by the substitution.

